%N A189359 Number of homogeneous games for n players.
%H A189359 Marnix Joris, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28133">Fishburn Numbers Count Homogeneous Games</a>, arXiv:2611.28133 [math.GM], 2026. See pp. 1, 3, 11.
%H A189359 I. Krohn and Peter Sudhölter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01415753">Directed and weighted majority games</a>, Mathematical Methods of Operation Research, 42(2) (1995), 189-216. See p. 213 (Table 1). See also <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/226788682">ResearchGate</a>.
%H A189359 Peter Sudhölter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01358803">Homogeneous games as anti step functions</a>, Int'l J. Game Theory 18 (1989), 433-469.
%F A189359 Conjecture: g.f.: Q(0)*x/(1-x), where Q(k) = 1 + (1-(1-x)^(2*k+2))/(1- (1-(1-x)^(2*k+3))/(1-(1-x)^(2*k+3) + 1/Q(k+1))); (continued fraction). - _Sergei N. Gladkovskii_, May 13 2013 [Proved by M. Joris (2026), Corollary 8.]
%F A189359 Note that a(n) - a(n-1) = A022493(n) for 1 <= n <= 9. Does this equality hold for n > 9? If so, then we have the g.f. 1/(1 - x)*( Sum_{n >= 1} Product_{k = 1..n} (1 - (1 - x)^k) ). - _Peter Bala_, Dec 13 2021 [The equality holds for all n >= 1, as proved by M. Joris (2026), Theorem 2.]
%F A189359 From _Marnix Joris_, Nov 24 2026: (Start)
%F A189359 a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n} A022493(k).
%F A189359 a(n) - a(n-1) = A022493(n) for n >= 1.
%F A189359 G.f.: 1/(1-x) * Sum_{n>=1} Product_{k=1..n} (1-(1-x)^k).
%F A189359 a(n) ~ A022493(n) ~ (12*sqrt(3)/Pi^(5/2))*exp(Pi^2/12)*(6/Pi^2)^n*n!*sqrt(n).
%F A189359 Limit_{n->infinity} a(n+1)/(n*a(n)) = 6/Pi^2.
%F A189359 a(5*n+2) == a(5*n+3) == a(5*n+4) (mod 5) for n >= 0.
%F A189359 a(5^r*n-3) == a(5^r*n-2) == a(5^r*n-1) (mod 5^r) for r >= 1 and n >= 1. (End)
%Y A189359 Subclass of A000617. Cf. A001532, A022493, A109456, A132183.
